# =============================================================================
# Environment template — copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#   npm run db:start        # prints the local URL and keys
#   npm run db:status       # prints them again at any time
#
# NEVER put real values in this file. NEVER commit .env.local.
#
# Three tiers, and the boundary between them matters:
#
#   LOCAL          Generated by the Supabase CLI on your machine. Disposable.
#   BROWSER-SAFE   NEXT_PUBLIC_* — compiled into the client bundle. Assume the
#                  whole internet can read these. Always constrained by RLS.
#   SERVER-ONLY    Privileged. Bypasses RLS. Read from Secret Manager at runtime
#                  in Cloud Run. A production server-only key must never exist on
#                  a development machine, in this repo, in GitHub, in Notion, in
#                  a prompt, or in a client bundle.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BROWSER-SAFE (local values)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local Supabase API URL. `npm run db:status` -> "API URL".
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# Browser-safe key, subject to RLS.
# Supabase is mid-migration between two naming schemes; supply whichever your
# stack prints. The app prefers PUBLISHABLE and falls back to ANON.
#   current: `npm run db:status` -> "publishable key" (sb_publishable_...)
#   legacy:  `npm run db:status` -> "anon key"        (a JWT)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-ONLY (local values)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Privileged key. BYPASSES RLS. Used only by local admin scripts.
# Do not prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Do not point this at production.
#   current: `npm run db:status` -> "secret key"        (sb_secret_...)
#   legacy:  `npm run db:status` -> "service_role key"  (a JWT)
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The service layer's direct PostgreSQL connection. BYPASSES RLS.
#
# This is a SECOND privileged credential, not the same one as the key above.
# The key above is presented to the Data API, which connects as `authenticator`
# and switches to `service_role`. This is a PostgreSQL login in its own right,
# with whatever attributes and grants that login has — locally, `postgres`,
# which owns every table and has admin privileges. It exists because real
# `begin`/`commit` needs a real connection; see
# docs/adr/0014-transactional-data-access.md.
#
# Local only, and enforced: src/lib/db/url.ts refuses any non-loopback host and
# any hosted Supabase connection string. Leave this blank and the same local
# default below is used.
#   `npm run db:status` -> "DB URL"   (postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres)
#
# The hosted equivalent — which PostgreSQL role, which grants, whether it
# bypasses RLS, and which pooling mode — is DELIBERATELY NOT DECIDED. LAN-83
# owns it. Do not put a hosted value here, or anywhere in this repository.
DATABASE_URL=

# Pool tuning. Left configurable, not hard-coded, because the hosted connection
# mode is not chosen yet and transaction-mode pooling forbids prepared
# statements. Blank uses the working local defaults (10 / 10000 / 5000).
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=
DATABASE_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=
DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL ONLY — the single pre-provisioned test user (`npm run db:seed-user`)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
TEST_USER_EMAIL=test.user@oxfordlancers.local
# Minimum 12 characters. Invent one locally; it is not shared and not a secret
# anyone else needs to know.
TEST_USER_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTOMATED DELIVERY — LAN-78
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Official 1:1 WhatsApp Business Platform messaging, through the Meta Cloud API
# (LAN-92). Read only by src/lib/delivery/config.ts, and never by anything else.
#
# A missing value is a REFUSAL, not a default: an unconfigured deployment
# records a failed, retryable delivery attempt naming the settings that are
# absent — by name, never by value — and sends nothing. Every developer machine
# and every CI run is unconfigured, and that is the expected state.
#
# The two halves resolve independently. Sending needs the first four; receiving
# a callback needs the last two. An outbound-only deployment is legitimate and
# is what the non-production test path is, while LAN-93 still owes a public
# webhook endpoint.

# Where this deployment answers, with no trailing slash. The RSVP link is built
# from it, which is why no host is hard-coded anywhere in the application.
#   local: http://localhost:3010    hosted: https://…
APP_BASE_URL=

# SENDING. The phone-number identifier is not secret; the access token is.
# In Cloud Run the token comes from Secret Manager at runtime and must never
# exist in this repository, in a commit, in Linear, in a log or in a prompt.
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# The approved template's name. An event invitation is business-initiated, so
# Meta requires an approved template — free-form text is delivered only inside a
# 24-hour window the recipient opened, which never applies here. The template
# takes four body parameters, in this order: invitee name, event name, when,
# RSVP URL.
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=

# RECEIVING. Both secret. The app secret verifies X-Hub-Signature-256 on every
# inbound callback; the verify token is echoed during Meta's subscription
# handshake.
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# Optional, with safe non-secret defaults:
#   WHATSAPP_GRAPH_BASE_URL        https://graph.facebook.com
#   WHATSAPP_GRAPH_VERSION         v21.0
#   DELIVERY_DEFAULT_CALLING_CODE  44
WHATSAPP_GRAPH_BASE_URL=
WHATSAPP_GRAPH_VERSION=
DELIVERY_DEFAULT_CALLING_CODE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL ONLY — proving the adapter against Meta's test number
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both are read ONLY when APP_BASE_URL is a loopback host, parsed rather than
# pattern-matched. In any deployed environment they resolve to "no override,
# template mode" whatever is set here, and src/lib/delivery/config.test.ts
# asserts exactly that. They exist because Meta's test number cannot reach the
# club's synthetic roster — those numbers are in Ofcom's reserved drama range —
# and cannot send free-form text without an open window.
#
# The recipient is a real phone number and therefore real personal data. Keep it
# in .env.local, which is git-ignored, and out of commits, Linear and logs.
WHATSAPP_TEST_RECIPIENT=
# `text` sends free-form text carrying the link; anything else sends the
# approved template. Loopback only.
WHATSAPP_MESSAGE_MODE=
